---

### Step 2 — Connect Source Database

*(If MongoDB → PostgreSQL was chosen, the source is MongoDB. If the reverse, the source is PostgreSQL.)*

**Sub-heading:** "Step 2 of 8 — Connect Source Database (MongoDB)"

**What the user sees:**
- A form with the heading: **"Enter your MongoDB connection details"**
- Two input options (user can toggle between them using tabs):

  **Tab 1: Connection String**
  - A text input field: *"mongodb://username:password@host:27017/dbname"*
  - A small eye icon to show/hide the password part of the string
  - A note: *"Supports MongoDB Atlas (mongodb+srv://...) and local connections"*

  **Tab 2: Individual Fields**
  - Host field (default: `localhost`)
  - Port field (default: `27017`)
  - Username field
  - Password field (masked)
  - Database Name field

- A **"Use a Saved Connection"** dropdown at the top — if the user has saved connections before, they can pick one and all fields are filled automatically.
- Button: **"Test Connection & Read Schema"** (filled, primary)

**What happens when the button is clicked:**
- The button changes to a loading state: *"Connecting…"* with a spinner
- The app pings the database
- **If connection fails:** A red error card appears below the button: *"❌ Could not connect. Error: [exact error message]"* — user must fix their credentials
- **If connection succeeds:** The button area is replaced with a green success card:
  - ✅ **"Connected!"**
  - *"Found 5 collections: users, orders, products, categories, reviews"*
  - A collapsible list showing a preview of the schema for each collection:
    - `users → { _id (ObjectId), name (String), email (String), address (Object), createdAt (Date) }`
    - `orders → { _id (ObjectId), userId (ObjectId), items (Array), totalAmount (Number) }`
    - *(and so on for all collections)*
  - A **"🗺️ View ERD Diagram"** button → opens a modal showing the visual relationship diagram of all collections

**🧠 AI Schema Health Score (appears automatically below the schema preview):**

Immediately after the schema is read, the app silently sends it to AI and displays a health score card (takes 3–5 seconds):

```
🧠 AI Schema Health Score: 71 / 100
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
🔴 -15pts  "orders.items" has inconsistent object structure
           (23% of documents have different field names in items array)
🟡 -10pts  "users.phone" has 3 different data types across documents
           (Integer: 60%, String: 35%, Null: 5%)
🟡 -4pts   "products" collection has no indexes at all
           (Full table scans will be slow after migration)
✅  Good:   "users.email" present in 99.8% of documents
✅  Good:   "orders" has a compound index on {userId, createdAt}

💡 Tip: Fix the "phone" type inconsistency before migrating
   for a smoother experience.
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[Dismiss]   [Copy Health Report]
```

If AI is unavailable, the health score is skipped silently and no card is shown.

**🔍 Partial Migration Selector (optional, collapsed by default):**

Below the health score, there is a collapsible section:

> **"⚙️ Advanced: Migrate only part of this database"** *(click to expand)*

By default this section is **collapsed** — meaning the whole database is migrated. If the user expands it, they see:

**Sub-section A — Choose Collections:**
- A list of all collections with a checkbox next to each one. All checkboxes are **ticked by default.**
- A small note: *"All collections are selected by default. Uncheck any you want to skip."*
- Example:
  ```
  ✅ users          (2,000 documents)
  ✅ orders         (5,000 documents)
  ✅ products       (500 documents)
  ✅ categories     (50 documents)
  ☐  inventory_logs (10,000 documents)  ← user unchecked this
  ✅ reviews        (3,000 documents)
  ```
- A **"Select All"** and **"Deselect All"** link at the top of the list.

**Sub-section B — Filter by Date (optional):**
- A toggle: **"Filter by document creation date"** (off by default)
- When toggled ON, two date pickers appear:
  - **From date:** *(date picker)*
  - **To date:** *(date picker — defaults to today)*
- A note: *"Only documents with a createdAt / created_at / timestamp field within this range will be migrated."*
- A preview updates automatically: *"Estimated documents matching filter: ~1,240 out of 5,000 orders"*

If the partial migration selector is left in its default state (all collections, no date filter), the migration proceeds exactly as normal.

  - A **"Next →"** button appears at the bottom right

**Checkbox option:**
- "💾 Save this connection for future use" — if checked, a small label field appears: *"Connection name"* where they type a friendly name like "My Local MongoDB"

